Certify that a target LLM `L` correctly answers multiple-choice questions derived from the PrimeKG precision medicine knowledge graph, loaded from file via `getGraph("primekg.csv")`. Each question asks which drug treats one disease and is contraindicated for another. A subgraph is sampled by first sampling a drug node `d`, then sampling a neighbour of `d` connected by a "treat" edge as `dis1`, then sampling a neighbour of `d` connected by a "contraindicate" edge as `dis2`. The question is formed from the template "Which drug treats {dis1} and is contraindicated for {dis2}?" and the ground truth answer is the name attribute of `d`.

The prompt also includes as context the last attribute of each of `d`, `dis1`, and `dis2` concatenated together, and four multiple choice options. Start with the ordered set `{d, dist1, dist2, dist3}` where `dist1`, `dist2`, `dist3` are three distractor drug nodes sampled one at a time from the graph, each must be unique and must not belong to the set of all drugs that also treat `dis1` and are contraindicated for `dis2`. Randomly shuffle this ordered set by sampling its elements one at a time without replacement using `meas` to produce a shuffled ordered set `options`. Label the shuffled elements "A)", "B)", "C)", "D)" in order. The ground truth is the label assigned to `d` in the shuffled set.

The prompt sent to `L` is the concatenation of the context, the question, and the formatted options. An external judge `J_QA` takes `L`'s response and the ground truth label and returns 1 if they match. Use 1000 samples at 95% confidence with the Clopper-Pearson certifier.